Sparky Stereo OS
----------------

This is the stereo version of the Linux® kernel by Sparky Stereo OS, forked from
torvalds/linux (https://github.com/torvalds/linux). It is the distribution's
kernel: HDMI 1.4a 3D modes and HDMI deep colour in the display drivers, and
fixes the distribution carries until they are merged.

Where it comes from:

* The Linux kernel (https://kernel.org) was started by Linus Torvalds and is
  developed by its community; see CREDITS and MAINTAINERS.
* Debian (https://www.debian.org/) is the base of the system.
* SparkyLinux (https://sparkylinux.org/), by Paweł "pavroo" Pijanowski, builds
  on Debian.
* Sparky Stereo OS (https://github.com/Sparky-OS/sparky-stereo-os) is the stereo
  3D edition of SparkyLinux: SparkyOS, powered by Debian.

The stereo3d branch holds the version the distribution builds. The licence is
unchanged; see COPYING. Linux® is the registered trademark of Linus Torvalds in
the U.S. and other countries.

How its nouveau differs
-----------------------

The nouveau in this tree is nouveau as its developers ship it, together with
work on its way upstream, each change with its authors' credit. Its HDMI 3D
output itself dates from 2017: Alastair Bridgewater's series, commit
0f18d2765ab1, reviewed on the list by Ilia Mirkin. Carried here:

* Mohamed Ahmed's display series: armed head state readback, output scaling,
  GB20x tile and phywin ownership, IMP mode validation and usage bounds
  through GSP, the Turing memory clock scanout workaround, the max bpc
  property, HDMI deep colour, and the link and bpc debugfs files.
* The NULL pointer fixes by Jim Cromie and Zhenhao Wan.

It differs from nouveau in one thing: it makes the driver respect the HDMI 3D
specification and the signal properties 3D depends on. Each change cites the
section it follows:

* drm: list and time HDMI 3D side by side (full). HDMI 1.4b, the HDMI
  Vendor-Specific Data Block's 3D_Structure_ALL bit 3 and 3D_Structure value
  3; both eyes at full width, blanking kept, the refresh rate unchanged, as
  NVIDIA's open timing library derives it. Compile-tested.
* nouveau, i915 and amdgpu: side by side (full) handled like frame packing in
  mode checks and in the head's input viewport. Compile-tested.
* nouveau: "Broadcast RGB" and content type on HDMI from Turing on. CTA-861,
  the AVI InfoFrame's quantization range and content type; limited range
  16-235 programmed as NVIDIA's open driver does. Compile-tested.
* nouveau: CD=5 in the General Control Packet at 10 bits per colour. HDMI 1.4b
  section 6.5.3. Tested on an RTX 3060 with two Sony 3D televisions.
* nouveau: 16 bits per colour (DC_48) on Turing and newer. HDMI deep colour,
  the General Control Packet's colour depth CD=7. Untested: no 48-bit display
  at hand.

Some of these are not 3D themselves: they came from making 3D modes work, and
they serve the same purpose, a driver that respects the specifications its 3D
modes depend on.

Sparky Stereo OS, Daniel Ramos's edition of SparkyLinux (by Paweł "pavroo" Pijanowski).


Linux kernel
============

The Linux kernel is the core of any Linux operating system. It manages hardware,
system resources, and provides the fundamental services for all other software.

Quick Start
-----------

* Report a bug: See Documentation/admin-guide/reporting-issues.rst
* Get the latest kernel: https://kernel.org
* Build the kernel: See Documentation/admin-guide/quickly-build-trimmed-linux.rst
* Join the community: https://lore.kernel.org/

Essential Documentation
-----------------------

All users should be familiar with:

* Building requirements: Documentation/process/changes.rst
* Code of Conduct: Documentation/process/code-of-conduct.rst
* License: See COPYING

Documentation can be built with make htmldocs or viewed online at:
https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/


Who Are You?
============

Find your role below:

* New Kernel Developer: Getting started with kernel development
* Academic Researcher: Studying kernel internals and architecture
* Security Expert: Hardening and vulnerability analysis
* Backport/Maintenance Engineer: Maintaining stable kernels
* System Administrator: Configuring and troubleshooting
* Maintainer: Leading subsystems and reviewing patches
* Hardware Vendor: Writing drivers for new hardware
* Distribution Maintainer: Packaging kernels for distros
* AI Coding Assistant: LLMs and AI-powered development tools


